      "entity_detection": {
        "_note": "German cannot use the check the other languages use. Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters: every noun is capitalised, so capitalisation cannot separate a common noun from a proper one; and the cited fields are English (rule text, methodology, field names), so an ordinary German noun never matches the input no matter how faithful the sentence is. Requiring every capitalised token to be grounded therefore rejected 100% of live German output on 2026-08-09 — 0/12 baseline.\n\nWhat is used instead: a RUN of two or more adjacent capitalised tokens where at least one is ungrounded. German separates ordinary nouns with articles, prepositions and verbs, so adjacent capitalised content words are characteristically a proper name — 'Sammlung Rosenfeld', 'Galerie Fischer'. A single ungrounded noun mid-sentence is not flagged, which is the accepted cost of keeping the layer able to produce German at all.\n\nUncited historical narrative is handled separately, by historical_elaboration_terms, which is the right home for it: those terms name Nazi-era practices rather than ordinary vocabulary.",
        "method": "adjacent_capitalised_run",
        "minimum_run_length": 2
      },
      "positional_capitals": {
        "_note": "German capitalises the first word of a sentence AND of a clause after a colon, so ordinary verbs, interrogatives and articles appear capitalised for purely positional reasons. None of them is ever part of a proper name, so they cannot contribute to a name-run. Without this, 'Ungeklärt: War Cordes, H. ...' read 'War Cordes' as a name and rejected a faithful sentence — found in live validation, 2026-08-09.",
