  "_entity_grounding": "A generated token that names something must be GROUNDED: it has to trace back to the cited fields. Three ways a token can be grounded — it appears in the fields; it is a cross-language form of something that appears in the fields (see equivalence_groups); or it is ordinary task vocabulary (see restatement_vocabulary). Anything else is the model supplying material of its own.",
  "equivalence_groups": {
    "_note": "Cross-language forms of the same referent. These do NOT permit a token on their own — a token is permitted only when another member of its group appears in the cited fields. Writing 'Allemagne' for a record that says 'Germany' is a translation of the input, not an addition to it; writing 'Allemagne' for a record that never mentions Germany is still a violation. Added after live validation showed French and Italian output being rejected almost entirely for correctly translating place names present in the record.",
